    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures \u2014 the project has a single acceptance test class, `AcceptanceTest`, whose new `coreFlagsPossibleDuplicate` case exercises this rule).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe soft-duplicate rule needed to be computed at create time and read back via a later `GET /api/owners/{id}`, so the result had to be **persisted** on the owner.\n\n1. **`Owner` model** \u2014 added persisted `possibleDuplicate` (`Boolean`, `possible_duplicate` column) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (`Integer`, `possible_duplicate_of` column) with getters/setters, alongside the existing `namesakeCount`/`bulkSignupWarning` derived fields.\n\n2. **DB schemas** \u2014 added the two columns to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by the test profile \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres for parity).\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `possibleDuplicate` and `possibleDuplicateOf` as `readOnly` properties on the `Owner` schema. This regenerates `OwnerDto` with matching fields, which MapStruct maps automatically from the entity (no explicit `@Mapping` needed), so both the POST response and the GET response carry them.\n\n4. **New function `CheckPossibleDuplicate`** \u2014 scans existing owners for one sharing `lastName` (case-insensitive) and `postcode` while having a *different* (E.164-normalized) telephone. If found, sets `possibleDuplicate = true` and `possibleDuplicateOf` to the earliest matching owner's id; otherwise `possibleDuplicate = false` and leaves the id unset. It runs on the built entity, skips self, and requires both lastName and postcode to be present.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `possibleDuplicate` step (under `govern: [ transaction ]`) right after `identity` (the hard-duplicate `CheckUniqueIdentity`, which 409s exact matches) and before `save`, so soft matches are still created and the new owner isn't compared against itself.",
